      'A <strong>normalized FFMI of 21–23</strong> combined with low body fat (typically 10–15%) usually produces a visibly lean, muscular appearance. Values around <strong>24–25</strong> represent an advanced, elite natural level. Individual appearance also depends on bone structure, muscle insertions, and body-fat distribution — not just FFMI.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Can you have an FFMI over 25 without steroids?',
    answer:
      '<strong>Yes.</strong> Research on drug-free sumo wrestlers and American football players has documented natural FFMI values well over 25. The original 25 value from Kouri et al. (1995) was a well-defined upper value within that specific sample of male athletes, not a universal biological ceiling. A single FFMI number cannot determine whether someone uses performance-enhancing drugs.',
  },
  {
    question: 'What is a good FFMI for a natural bodybuilder?',
    answer:
      'A competitive natural bodybuilder typically aims for a <strong>normalized FFMI of 23–25 on stage</strong>. For most male natural lifters, 20–21.9 is good and 22–23.9 is excellent. For female natural lifters, 17–17.9 is good and 18–18.9 is excellent.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Does creatine increase FFMI?',
    answer:
      '<strong>Yes, temporarily — for some people.</strong> Creatine increases intracellular water retention in muscle tissue, which increases total fat-free mass. This can boost FFMI in some individuals, though the size of any change depends on individual response, training status, and how body composition is measured. This increase mostly reflects muscle water content rather than newly built muscle tissue. True muscle growth from creatine only occurs when combined with resistance training.',
  },
  {
    question: 'What is the natural limit for FFMI?',
    answer:
      'The often-cited <strong>25</strong> comes from Kouri et al. (1995), which reported a well-defined upper value around 25 among the non-steroid-using men in that study. Later research has documented natural values above 25 in specific athletic populations, so 25 should be treated as a research reference rather than an absolute ceiling.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Does FFMI include bone weight?',
    answer:
      '<strong>Yes.</strong> FFMI uses fat-free mass, which includes bone mineral, organs, body water, connective tissue, and muscle. It does not isolate skeletal muscle mass. This is why FFMI should not be interpreted as a direct measure of muscle mass.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Why do tall lifters need Normalized FFMI?',
    answer:
      'Raw FFMI does not scale perfectly across different heights. Normalized FFMI applies the height correction proposed by Kouri et al. relative to a <strong>1.80 m reference</strong>, helping improve comparisons between individuals of different statures.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Do women use the FFMI scale?',
    answer:
      '<strong>Yes.</strong> FFMI can be calculated for women using the same formula. However, the commonly cited 25 came from a male sample and should not be applied to women. Female collegiate-athlete research has reported upper 97.5th-percentile FFMI thresholds around <strong>23.90 kg/m²</strong>.',
  },
  {
    question: 'How accurate is an FFMI calculator?',
    answer:
      'The arithmetic is straightforward, but FFMI is only as accurate as the body-fat estimate used. Different methods — DEXA, skinfolds, tape-measure equations, and bioelectrical impedance — can produce meaningfully different body-fat percentages. For a starting estimate, use our <a href="/tools/nutrition/body-fat-calculator/">Body Fat Calculator</a>.',
  },
  {
    question: 'What should I calculate after my FFMI?',
    answer:
      'Use our <a href="/tools/nutrition/lean-body-mass-calculator/">Lean Body Mass Calculator</a> for the underlying lean-mass number, our <a href="/tools/nutrition/body-fat-calculator/">Body Fat Calculator</a> for body-composition context, or our <a href="/tools/nutrition/protein-calculator/">Protein Calculator</a> for daily protein targets based on your goal.',
