    ))}
  </article>

  <article class="article-section">
    <h2>Sources &amp; Methodology</h2>

    <p>
      RepMaths uses FFMI as an educational body-composition metric. It is
      not a medical diagnosis and it is not a definitive test for
      performance-enhancing drug use.
    </p>

    <ul>
      <li>
        Kouri EM, Pope HG Jr, Katz DL, Oliva P.
        <em>Fat-free mass index in users and nonusers of
        anabolic-androgenic steroids.</em>
        <a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/7496846/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">PubMed</a>.
      </li>
      <li>
        Harty PS et al.
        <em>Upper and lower thresholds of fat-free mass index in a large
        cohort of female collegiate athletes.</em>
        <a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/31238804/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">PubMed</a>.
      </li>
      <li>
        Blue MNM et al.
        <em>Normative fat-free mass index values for a diverse sample of
        collegiate female athletes.</em>
        <a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/30893018/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">PubMed</a>.
      </li>
      <li>
        <em>Fat-Free Mass Index in NCAA Division I and II Collegiate
        American Football Players.</em>
        <a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/27930454/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">PubMed</a>.
      </li>
      <li>
        Schutz Y, Kyle UU, Pichard C.
        <em>Fat-free mass index and fat mass index percentiles in
        Caucasians aged 18–98 y.</em>
        <a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/12080404/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">PubMed</a>.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </article>

